               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Image of a chocolate pot found in Cahal Pech, Belize*",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }

         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "aad23fe2-76a0-45af-bf8c-c13ffe7e536c",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "aad23fe2-76a0-45af-bf8c-c13ffe7e536c",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=7a462cd1-cfd1-4ac6-bde8-e8a64092388f",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2024",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "When humans first began to think abstractly, we move from a place without counting to markers that help us count. In other words, we move from a spoken and imagined situation to something we can touch.\n\nWould it be possible to do the reverse with numbers and imagine something so large that we need a single symbol to represent it?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "ddb81ec9-48a7-4959-9ca2-fa40132140fe",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "yes",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "ddb81ec9-48a7-4959-9ca2-fa40132140fe",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "shocking",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=7a462cd1-cfd1-4ac6-bde8-e8a64092388f",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2024",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Walking in One Direction\n\nNothing stops us from continuing to add numbers. Given this fact, a person could spend an entire lifetime naming numbers and there would still be numbers to name. So, we also need to do something about them, just as we did when we created symbols to give them names.\n\nSince we know that numbers grow as we add more digits to the left, we can say that they go in one direction. Or, more precisely, that they tend toward a destination.\n\nWith this, as numbers grow larger and larger, we will no longer name a number; we will say that numbers tend toward infinity, and we represent it as ∞.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "609397d1-d852-4926-b5e3-0f123503bed6",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "00da5320-730e-43a6-976c-46494ad02a66",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=7a462cd1-cfd1-4ac6-bde8-e8a64092388f",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2024",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we learned that around us we have small quantities that can be counted with the numbers we have already learned.\n\nWe also saw that there is no rule that stops numbers from growing; therefore, we need to go further to define very large quantities.\n\nFinally, we reached the conclusion that, as things grow very large, they tend toward infinity (∞), which is not a number.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
